ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection
==================================================
A sexually transmitted infection (STI), also referred to as a sexually transmitted disease (STD) and the older term venereal disease (VD), is an  [LINK: (infection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infection]  that is  [LINK: (spread) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transmission_(medicine)]  by  [LINK: (sexual activity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_sexual_activity] , especially  [LINK: (vaginal intercourse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexual_intercourse] ,  [LINK: (anal sex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anal_sex] ,  [LINK: (oral sex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oral_sex] , or sometimes  [LINK: (manual sex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-penetrative_sex#Manual_sex] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-Women2017-5]  [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-6]  STIs often do not initially cause symptoms, [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  which results in a risk of transmitting them to others. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-7]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-8]  The term sexually transmitted infection is generally preferred over sexually transmitted disease or venereal disease, as it includes cases with no  [LINK: (symptomatic disease) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signs_and_symptoms#Symptomatic] . [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2003Term-9]  Symptoms and signs of STIs may include  [LINK: (vaginal discharge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaginal_discharge] ,  [LINK: (penile discharge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penile_discharge] ,  [LINK: (ulcers on or around the genitals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genital_ulcers] , and  [LINK: (pelvic pain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelvic_pain] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Some STIs can cause  [LINK: (infertility) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infertility] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]

Bacterial STIs include  [LINK: (chlamydia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlamydia_infection] ,  [LINK: (gonorrhea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gonorrhea] , and  [LINK: (syphilis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syphilis] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Viral STIs include  [LINK: (genital warts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genital_warts] ,  [LINK: (genital herpes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genital_herpes] , and  [LINK: (HIV/AIDS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HIV/AIDS] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Parasitic STIs include  [LINK: (trichomoniasis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trichomoniasis] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Most STIs are treatable and curable; of the most common infections, syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydia, and trichomoniasis are curable, while HIV/AIDS and genital herpes are not curable. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Some  [LINK: (vaccinations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccinations]  may decrease the risk of certain infections including  [LINK: (hepatitis B) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hepatitis_B_vaccine]  and a few types of  [LINK: (HPV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HPV_vaccine] . [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-CDC2013P-2]   [LINK: (Safe sex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safe_sex]  practices such as the use of  [LINK: (condoms) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Condom] , having smaller number of sexual partners, and being in a relationship in which each person only has sex with the other also decreases STIs risk. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-CDC2013P-2]   [LINK: (Comprehensive sex education) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comprehensive_sex_education]  may also be useful. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-10]

STI diagnostic tests are usually easily available in the  [LINK: (developed world) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Developed_world] , but they are often unavailable in the  [LINK: (developing world) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Developing_world] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  There is often shame and  [LINK: (stigma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_stigma]  associated with STIs. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  In 2015, STIs other than HIV resulted in 108,000 deaths worldwide. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-GBD2015De-4]  Globally, in 2015, about 1.1 billion people had STIs other than HIV/AIDS. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-GBD2015Pre-3]  About 500 million have either syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydia or trichomoniasis. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  At least an additional 530 million have genital herpes, and 290 million women have  [LINK: (human papillomavirus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_papillomavirus] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-WHO2014-1]  Historical documentation of STIs in  [LINK: (antiquity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_history]  dates back to at least the  [LINK: (Ebers Papyrus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebers_Papyrus]  (c. 1550 BCE) and the  [LINK: (Hebrew Bible) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebrew_Bible] / [LINK: (Old Testament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Testament]  (8th/7th C. BCE). [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-11]


# SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS #

Not all STIs are  [LINK: (symptomatic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symptom] , and symptoms may not appear immediately after infection. In some instances a disease can be carried with no symptoms, which leaves a greater risk of passing the disease on to others. Depending on the disease, some untreated STIs can lead to  [LINK: (infertility) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infertility] ,  [LINK: (chronic pain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronic_pain]  or death. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-STI_video-12]

The presence of an STI in  [LINK: (prepubescent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prepubescent]  children may indicate  [LINK: (sexual abuse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexual_abuse] . [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-Hoffman2012-13]


# CAUSE #

A sexually transmitted infection present in a pregnant woman may be passed on to the infant before or after birth. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexually_transmitted_infection#cite_note-14]

